19:["$","$L1a","overview",{"problem":{"id":"overview","patternId":"bit-manipulation","title":"Overview","subtitle":"AND · OR · XOR · shifts · the classic tricks","kind":"concept","input":[0,1,0,1],"approaches":[{"label":"The bit toolkit","complexity":{"time":"O(1) per op","space":"O(1)"},"pseudocode":["// bit b worth 2^position","x & y   // 1 where both 1   (mask / test)","x | y   // 1 where either   (set bits)","x ^ y   // 1 where differ   (x^x=0, x^0=x)","x << k  // shift up (×2^k);  1<<k = bit-k mask","n & (n-1)  // clears the lowest set bit"],"steps":[{"codeLine":1,"narration":"Every integer is a string of BITS, each worth a power of two: positions (left→right here) are 8, 4, 2, 1. 5 = 0101 = 4 + 1. Bit tricks manipulate these directly with the operators &, |, ^, ~, and the shifts << / >>.","arrValues":[0,1,0,1],"highlights":[1,3],"vars":[["value",5],["binary","0101"],["places","8 4 2 1"]]},{"codeLine":2,"narration":"AND (&) keeps a bit only where BOTH are 1, a mask/intersection. 5 & 3 = 0101 & 0011 = 0001 = 1. A common use: x & 1 tests the lowest bit (odd/even).","arrValues":[0,0,0,1],"highlights":[3],"vars":[["5","0101"],["3","0011"],["5 & 3","0001 = 1"]]},{"codeLine":3,"narration":"OR (|) sets a bit where EITHER is 1, a union. 5 | 3 = 0101 | 0011 = 0111 = 7. Used to TURN ON specific bits via a mask.","arrValues":[0,1,1,1],"highlights":[1,2,3],"vars":[["5","0101"],["3","0011"],["5 | 3","0111 = 7"]]},{"codeLine":4,"narration":"XOR (^) sets a bit where the two DIFFER. Its magic: x ^ x = 0 and x ^ 0 = x, so XOR is its own inverse and pairs CANCEL. This single fact solves Single Number, Missing Number, swapping without a temp, and more.","arrValues":[0,1,1,0],"highlights":[1,2],"vars":[["5 ^ 3","0110 = 6"],["x ^ x",0],["x ^ 0","x"]]},{"codeLine":5,"narration":"SHIFTS move all bits. x << 1 appends a 0 (×2): 5 << 1 = 1010 = 10. x >> 1 drops the lowest bit (÷2). Shifts build masks like 1 << k = \"only bit k set\".","arrValues":[1,0,1,0],"highlights":[0,2],"vars":[["5 << 1","1010 = 10"],["1 << 2","0100"]]},{"codeLine":6,"narration":"The most-used trick: n & (n − 1) clears the LOWEST set bit. 6 = 0110, 5 = 0101, 6 & 5 = 0100, the rightmost 1 vanished. Repeating it until n = 0 counts set bits in O(#set bits). Keep these tricks handy; the problems just compose them.","arrValues":[0,1,0,0],"highlights":[1],"vars":[["6","0110"],["6 & 5","0100"],["effect","drops lowest 1"]]}]}]},"patternTitle":"Bit Manipulation"}]
